 ## 9. Socastee: a historic village on the waterway
 Socastee began in the late 1800s as a lumber and turpentine camp. Its old swing bridge, built in the mid-1930s, carries Dick Pond Road over the Intracoastal Waterway. The bridge, two houses, a store and a pecan grove make up a historic district on the National Register.
+The waterway past Socastee is a dug canal: in the 1930s, crews dredged about 34 miles, much of it through high land from Little River to Socastee Creek. The county's Socastee Landing and Park, at 4299 Peachtree Road, has a public boat landing on the Intracoastal Waterway. Socastee has its own branch of the county library, on the 707 Connector.
